meta.docs:
An invoice editor in a modal: the invoice number, who it is billed to (name and email, edited in place), the issue and due dates (Figma's text over the system date picker; "in 14 days" is read from the two), an editable table of lines (description, quantity, rate; each amount is quantity × rate), a card payments switch, and the subtotal, tax and total, all recomputed as you type. Add a line with "Add line" (or Enter on the last line), remove one with the × at the end of its description. "Save draft" hands the values over without validating; "Send invoice" validates, shows "Sending…", then marks the invoice sent, with "Edit invoice" to change it and "New invoice" to start the next one. Animated with anime.js: the modal rises in, the details and lines follow and the totals count up from zero, every amount counts to its new value, new lines unfold and removed ones fold away, the switch's knob slides, an invalid send shakes and the sent state pops in.

## Usage

With the sample content (the Figma frame):

```tsx
import { InvoiceForm } from "@/components/invoice-form"

export function Example() {
  return <InvoiceForm onSubmit={(values) => console.log(values)} />
}
```

With your own invoice, currency and locale, saving drafts and sending through your API:

```tsx
"use client"


import { InvoiceForm, invoiceTotals, type InvoiceFormValues } from "@/components/invoice-form"

async function post(path: string, values: InvoiceFormValues) {
  const response = await fetch(path, {
    method: "POST",
    headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
    body: JSON.stringify({ ...values, ...invoiceTotals(values.lines, values.taxRate) }),
  })
  // a rejected promise keeps the invoice open and shows "Couldn't send the invoice. Try again."
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Request failed: ${response.status}`)
}

export function Example({ onClose }: { onClose: () => void }) {
  return (
    <InvoiceForm
      title="Rechnung"
      locale="de-DE"
      currency="EUR"
      reminderDays={7}
      defaultValues={{
        number: "RE-2026-118",
        clientName: "Fahrwerk GmbH",
        clientEmail: "buchhaltung@fahrwerk.example",
        issued: "2026-11-02",
        due: "2026-12-02",
        lines: [{ description: "Wartungsvertrag, November", quantity: 1, rate: 1850 }],
        taxRate: 19,
      }}
      onSaveDraft={(values) => post("/api/invoices/drafts", values)}
      onSubmit={(values) => post("/api/invoices", values)}
      onClose={onClose}
    />
  )
}
```

Card payments only for teams on a paid plan (controlled switch):

```tsx
"use client"

import * as React from "react"

import { InvoiceForm } from "@/components/invoice-form"

export function Example({ onPaidPlan }: { onPaidPlan: boolean }) {
  const [cardPayments, setCardPayments] = React.useState(false)

  return (
    <InvoiceForm
      cardPayments={cardPayments}
      onCardPaymentsChange={(enabled) => {
        if (enabled && !onPaidPlan) return window.alert("Card payments are on the Pro plan.")
        setCardPayments(enabled)
      }}
    />
  )
}
```

## Props

| Prop | Type | Default | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `title` | `string` | `"Invoice"` | The header title; also the form's accessible name. |
| `defaultValues` | `Partial<InvoiceFormValues>` | No client, one empty line | Starting values. By default the client name and email are empty and there is one empty line; the number, dates and tax are Figma's. `sampleInvoiceValues` is Figma's filled sample (Acme Studio, three lines). "New invoice" starts from them with the next number (`INV-0427` → `INV-0428`). |
| `currency` | `string` | `"USD"` | ISO 4217 code. Whole amounts show without cents (`$2,400`), others with (`$422.40`). |
| `locale` | `string` | `"en-US"` | Formats amounts, quantities and dates. |
| `reminderDays` | `number \| null` | `3` | "Auto-reminder 3 days before due"; once sent, the footer names the reminder's date. `null` for none. |
| `cardPayments` / `onCardPaymentsChange` | `boolean` / `(enabled: boolean) => void` | `defaultValues.cardPayments` (`false`) | Controlled or uncontrolled card payments switch; its state is also in the submitted values. |
| `onSubmit` | `(values: InvoiceFormValues) => void \| Promise<void>` | – | "Send invoice", once everything is valid. While a returned promise is pending the button shows "Sending…" and the fields are disabled; a rejection shows an error in the footer. |
| `onSaveDraft` | `(values: InvoiceFormValues) => void \| Promise<void>` | – | "Save draft": called without validation; the button shows "Saving…", then "Saved". |
| `onClose` | `() => void` | starts a new invoice | The header's close button. |

`InvoiceFormValues` is `{ number, clientName, clientEmail, issued, due, lines: { description, quantity, rate }[], taxRate, cardPayments }`, dates as `YYYY-MM-DD`, `taxRate` in percent. Empty lines (no description, no rate) are left out. `invoiceTotals(lines, taxRate)` returns the `{ subtotal, tax, total }` the form shows, rounded to cents. Other props go to the `<form>` (`className`, `style`, `aria-*`, `ref`, `onKeyDown`). The file also exports `sampleInvoiceValues` and the types `InvoiceFormProps`, `InvoiceFormValues`, `InvoiceLine` and `InvoiceTotals`.

## Notes

- Light by default and dark under your app's `.dark` class: the form sets its own `color-scheme` (the date pickers follow it), and its palette holds `light-dark()` pairs from the Figma light 2713:8550 and dark 1891:79734 frames.
- Validation on send: a client name, a valid email, both dates with the due date on or after the issue date, at least one line, and a description and a quantity above 0 on every line that has content. Invalid fields get `aria-invalid` and a message linked with `aria-describedby`, the first one takes focus and the invoice shakes; a message goes away as soon as its field is fixed. A due date before the issue date is flagged as soon as you pick it.
- The table edits like a spreadsheet: a quantity or rate cell shows its plain number, selected, while you edit it (the amount and totals follow every keystroke) and its formatted value after; ArrowUp / ArrowDown step it by 1 (Shift: 10). Enter moves down a line and adds one after the last; Enter never sends the invoice. Tab goes description → quantity → rate → remove.
- The cell being edited is raised and boxed like Figma's, its border adding 2px to its line as drawn; the lines keep those 2px in reserve while nothing is edited, so the table's height never changes.
- Re-skin it through the CSS variables on the form: `--inv-surface`, `--inv-sunken`, `--inv-raised`, `--inv-selected`, `--inv-line`, `--inv-divider`, `--inv-text`, `--inv-text-2`, `--inv-text-3`, `--inv-accent`, `--inv-on-accent`, `--inv-ring`, `--inv-danger`, `--inv-success`, `--inv-shadow` (the same roles as the New Project form's `--np-*`).
- The form is as wide as its container up to 600px (the Figma width). Under 520px the dates sit under the client, each line wraps into two rows (description, then quantity, rate and amount with their names) and the totals go under the card payments box.
- Installs Geist and Geist Mono (`forms-font-geist`, `forms-font-geist-mono`).
- With `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` nothing animates; React always renders the final state, so the server render is complete too.
- Figma: form 1891:79735 ("Modal" in "Stage / 06 Editable table" 1891:79734, page "Page 7"). The error, sending, saved and sent states, the remove button, the switch's on state and the focus rings are designed in the form's own language. Deviations: the details' dividers run the full height (Figma's Issued column is 69px tall, so its divider stops 21px short), and the totals keep Figma's position although their frame is a fixed 10px with the rows overflowing into the section's padding.

A "Schedule a session" booking dialog: the host and the session's details on the left (duration, where it happens, the time zone), a week of days on the right with the open times of the picked day split into morning and afternoon, and a summary that follows the choice ("Thursday, 5 November · 10:30–11:00"). Days without an open time are dashed and explained under the strip ("Wed 4 is fully booked. Weekends are unavailable."), the arrows move a week (opening its first open day), and "Change time zone" re-reads every time, the summary and its date in another zone. "Confirm booking" checks that a time is picked, shows a spinner, then a confirmation with "Book another" and "Done". Animated with anime.js: the dialog rises in and its parts stagger, the light behind the details rises, a new week slides in from its side, a picked day pops and its times deal in, the slot's check draws, the summary slides to its new text, the stars twinkle while the form is on screen, a blocked confirm shakes, and the confirmation's check draws itself.

## Usage

With the sample content (the Figma frame):

```tsx
import { SessionSchedulingForm } from "@/components/session-scheduling-form"

export function Example() {
  return <SessionSchedulingForm today="2026-11-01" onSubmit={(values) => console.log(values)} />
}
```

With your own host, availability and time zones, booking through your API:

```tsx
"use client"

import { SessionSchedulingForm, getSessionStart, type SessionSchedulingFormValues, type SessionSlot } from "@/components/session-scheduling-form"

const times = ["08:30", "09:15", "11:00", "14:30", "16:45"]

// Closed on Fridays and weekends, 11:00 already taken every Tuesday.
function slotsFor(date: string): SessionSlot[] {
  const day = new Date(`${date}T00:00`).getDay()
  if (day === 0 || day === 5 || day === 6) return []
  return times.map((time) => ({ time, available: !(day === 2 && time === "11:00") }))
}

export function Example({ onDone }: { onDone: () => void }) {
  async function book(values: SessionSchedulingFormValues) {
    const start = getSessionStart(values, -300) // the host's slots are in New York time (UTC−5)
    const response = await fetch("/api/bookings", {
      method: "POST",
      headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
      body: JSON.stringify({ start: start?.toISOString(), timeZone: values.timeZone }),
    })
    // a throw keeps the form and shows "Couldn't book the session. Try again."
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Booking failed: ${response.status}`)
  }

  return (
    <SessionSchedulingForm
      title="Book a design review"
      description="45 minutes with a senior designer to go through your flows."
      host={{ name: "Mara Lindqvist", role: "Design lead" }}
      duration={45}
      location="Zoom · link in the invite"
      getSlots={slotsFor}
      timeZones={[
        { value: "EST", label: "Eastern Standard Time", offset: -300 },
        { value: "PST", label: "Pacific Standard Time", offset: -480 },
        { value: "GMT", label: "Greenwich Mean Time", offset: 0 },
      ]}
      defaultValues={{ date: "", time: "", timeZone: "EST" }}
      successMessage="Mara will send the agenda a day before."
      onSubmit={book}
      onCancel={onDone}
      onClose={onDone}
    />
  )
}
```

## Props

| Prop | Type | Default | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `title` | `string` | `"Schedule a session"` | The heading; also the form's accessible name. |
| `description` | `string` | the Figma sentence | Under the title. |
| `host` | `SessionHost` | Dana Okafor | `{ name, role?, initials?, avatarSrc? }`. Without `avatarSrc` the avatar shows the initials (from the name when left out) on the accent gradient. |
| `duration` | `number` | `30` | Minutes: "30 minutes", the summary's end time and "30 min with …". |
| `location` | `string` | `"Google Meet · link sent by email"` | The second detail line. |
| `getSlots` | `(date: string) => SessionSlot[]` | `sampleSessionSlots` | The start times on a day (`"YYYY-MM-DD"`), each `{ time: "HH:MM", available }`, in the host's time zone. `[]` is a closed day; a day whose slots are all taken is fully booked. |
| `today` | `Date \| string` | the current date | Days before it can't be booked; the previous-week arrow stops at its week. |
| `timeZones` | `SessionTimeZone[]` | `sampleSessionTimeZones` | `{ value, label, offset }` (`offset` in minutes from UTC). The first is the host's: slot times are written in it. |
| `defaultValues` | `Partial<SessionSchedulingFormValues>` | `sampleSessionSchedulingValues` | Starting `date`, `time` and `timeZone` (a time zone `value`). Cancel (without `onCancel`) goes back to them. |
| `values` / `onValuesChange` | `SessionSchedulingFormValues` / `(values) => void` | – | Controlled values; the strip follows a date the parent moves to another week. |
| `successMessage` | `string` | invite sentence | The confirmation's last line. |
| `onSubmit` | `(values: SessionSchedulingFormValues) => void \| Promise<void>` | – | Called by "Confirm booking" once a day and time are picked. While a returned promise is pending the button says "Booking…" and the form is inert; a rejection keeps the form and shows an error in the summary. |
| `onCancel` | `() => void` | resets the form | Cancel. |
| `onClose` | `() => void` | resets the form | "Done" on the confirmation ("Book another" keeps the day and clears the time). |

`SessionSchedulingFormValues` is `{ date, time, timeZone }`: `date` is `"YYYY-MM-DD"`, `time` the slot's start in the host's zone (`"HH:MM"`), `timeZone` the zone the guest picked. `getSessionStart(values, hostOffset?)` turns them into the start instant (a `Date`). Other props go to the `<form>` (`className`, `style`, `aria-*`, `ref`). The file also exports the sample data (`sampleSessionHost`, `sampleSessionSlots`, `sampleSessionTimeZones`, `sampleSessionSchedulingValues`) and the types `SessionSchedulingFormProps`, `SessionSchedulingFormValues`, `SessionSlot`, `SessionTimeZone` and `SessionHost`.

## Notes

- Validation runs on "Confirm booking": without a picked time the times get `aria-invalid`, the summary shows "Pick a time to book the session." (linked with `aria-describedby`), the first open time takes focus and the form shakes. The message goes as soon as a time is picked.
- Everything shown is read from the state: the month and "Week of 2 Nov", which days are dashed and the line under the strip (past days, fully booked days, closed days, "Weekends are unavailable."), the "Available times · Thu, 5 Nov" heading, the times in the picked zone (a time that moves to another day gets a small +1 / −1), the zone in the details and above the times, and the summary with its end time.
- The days and the times are radio groups (Radix): arrow keys move between open ones, taken ones are skipped. "Change time zone" is a Radix dropdown menu.
- Re-skin it through the CSS variables on the form, named after the Dark SaaS library's roles: `--sched-canvas`, `--sched-surface`, `--sched-surface-2`, `--sched-elevated`, `--sched-line`, `--sched-line-strong`, `--sched-ink`, `--sched-ink-2`, `--sched-muted`, `--sched-disabled`, `--sched-on-accent`, `--sched-accent` (focus rings, the zone menu's check), `--sched-danger`, `--sched-warning`, `--sched-success`, plus the Primary button's `--sched-cta-from` / `--sched-cta-to` (gradient), `--sched-cta-glow`, `--sched-cta-edge` (inner glow) and `--sched-cta-shadow`, the picked slot's `--sched-pick` / `--sched-pick-line`, `--sched-cta-rim` (the accent stroke's light), and the roles the light frame colours apart: `--sched-soft`, `--sched-caption`, `--sched-avatar-ink`, `--sched-day`, `--sched-day-ink`, `--sched-slot`, `--sched-slot-line`, `--sched-slot-off`, `--sched-time-ink`, `--sched-picked`, `--sched-picked-edge`, `--sched-summary`, the menu's `--sched-menu`, `--sched-menu-line`, `--sched-menu-shadow` and the details panel's `--sched-stars`, `--sched-pill-end`, `--sched-pill-rim`, `--sched-pill-rim-end`, `--sched-pill-shadow`, e.g. `style={{ "--sched-cta-from": "#2f6bff", "--sched-cta-to": "#5aa2ff", "--sched-cta-glow": "#2f6bff" } as React.CSSProperties}`.
- The form is as wide as its container up to 798px (the Figma width). Under 720px the details sit above the picker, and under 460px the times wrap three to a row.
- Installs Geist and Geist Mono (`forms-pro-font-geist`, `forms-pro-font-geist-mono`).
- The sample days are 2–8 November 2026: pass a `today` on or before 2 November 2026 (as the examples do) to keep the sample week bookable after that date.
- With `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` nothing animates; React always renders the final state, so the server render is complete too.
- Light by default and dark under your app's `.dark` class (the form sets its own color-scheme; its palette holds light-dark() pairs from the Figma light 2713:27641 and dark 1891:99548 frames). The light frame has no light beams behind the details, dark stars instead of white ones and a flat pink pill. Two of its texts are left as in dark there: the "Available times" heading stays #f5f5f5 (white on white), so it takes the light ink here, and "Morning" turned black while "Afternoon" stayed grey, so both time groups keep the grey.
- Figma: form 1891:99549 ("Scheduling dialog" in "02 — Appointment Scheduling" 1891:99548, page "Page 7", section "Premium ($14)"; light 2713:27641). The library's instances (Avatar, Date cell, Time slot, Time picker, Button) and icons are rebuilt in the file; the accent's grain image is an SVG noise, so no image ships. The error, booking and confirmation states, the zone menu, hover and focus states are designed in the form's own language. "Confirm booking" stays enabled without a time and explains what's missing (the library's note keeps it disabled); a taken time is dashed without the strikethrough the library's note mentions, as Figma draws it.

A new-product dialog for a store of digital goods: a cover image, a name, a price, how many licenses are for sale, tags, optional license tiers, then "Save draft" or Publish. The cover box takes a dropped image or opens the file picker (click, Enter or Space): it checks the type and file size (`coverTypes`, `coverMaxBytes`), previews the image in place with a remove button, shows its name and pixel size, and warns (without blocking) when it's smaller than `coverSize`; the hint "PNG, 1600 × 1200" is read from the same props. The price field has a currency picker (each currency with its sign, decimals and price range) and "You keep 95%" from `payoutShare`; typed prices like `1,299` are tidied to `1299.00` when you leave the field. The licenses stepper works with its buttons, the arrow keys, Page Up / Page Down and Home / End. Tags are added with "Add" (Enter or a comma adds, Escape closes, Backspace in the empty field removes the last one, a repeat makes the existing tag pulse) and removed with the × that appears over a tag on hover or focus. "Sell license tiers" opens Personal, Team and Extended prices that follow the price (×1, ×3, ×10) until you edit one. "Save draft" calls `onSaveDraft` with the values as they are and the footer's "Draft saved 2 min ago" counts from the last save. Publish validates (a cover, a name, a price in range, 1–9,999 licenses, every tier price), focuses the first problem and shakes the dialog, then shows a spinner and the product's link. Everything moves with anime.js: the dialog rises in with its fields staggered and its tags popping, the cover fades in, the currency sign and the license count roll, tags pop in and out while the others slide over, the switch's thumb springs and the tier prices unfold, and the confirmation draws its check.

## Usage

With the sample currencies and tiers, the product empty:

```tsx
import { NewProductForm } from "@/components/new-product-form"

export function Example() {
  return <NewProductForm onSubmit={(values) => console.log(values)} />
}
```

With your own currencies, tiers and payout share, an upload on publish and drafts saved to your API:

```tsx
"use client"

import {
  NewProductForm,
  type NewProductCurrency,
  type NewProductFormValues,
  type NewProductTier,
} from "@/components/new-product-form"

const currencies: NewProductCurrency[] = [
  { value: "EUR", symbol: "€", min: 2 },
  { value: "USD", symbol: "$", min: 2 },
]

const tiers: NewProductTier[] = [
  { value: "solo", label: "Solo", multiplier: 1 },
  { value: "studio", label: "Studio", multiplier: 4 },
]

function toFormData(values: NewProductFormValues) {
  const data = new FormData()
  if (values.cover) data.append("cover", values.cover)
  data.append("product", JSON.stringify({ ...values, cover: undefined }))
  return data
}

async function publish(values: NewProductFormValues) {
  const response = await fetch("/api/products", { method: "POST", body: toFormData(values) })
  // a throw keeps the dialog as it is and shows "Couldn't publish the product. Try again."
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Publish failed: ${response.status}`)
}

async function saveDraft(values: NewProductFormValues) {
  const response = await fetch("/api/products/drafts", { method: "PUT", body: toFormData(values) })
  // a throw shows "Couldn't save the draft. Try again." in the footer
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Save failed: ${response.status}`)
}

export function Example({ lastSaved }: { lastSaved: string | null }) {
  return (
    <NewProductForm
      title="List a template"
      description="It goes live in the shop when you publish."
      currencies={currencies}
      tiers={tiers}
      payoutShare={0.88}
      coverTypes={["image/png", "image/jpeg", "image/webp"]}
      coverSize={{ width: 2400, height: 1800 }}
      storeUrl="shop.example.com/t"
      draftSavedAt={lastSaved ? new Date(lastSaved) : null}
      defaultValues={{ currency: "EUR", licenses: 100 }}
      onSaveDraft={saveDraft}
      onSubmit={publish}
    />
  )
}
```

With the tiers behind an upgrade and the currency owned by the parent:

```tsx
"use client"

import * as React from "react"

import { NewProductForm } from "@/components/new-product-form"

export function Example() {
  const [currency, setCurrency] = React.useState("GBP")

  return (
    <NewProductForm
      currency={currency}
      onCurrencyChange={setCurrency}
      tiersLocked
      onUpgrade={() => window.location.assign("/settings/plan")}
      onSubmit={() => new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 800))}
    />
  )
}
```

## Props

| Prop | Type | Default | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `title` | `string` | `"New product"` | The heading; also the form's accessible name. |
| `description` | `string` | `"Add it to your store. You can publish later."` | The line under the heading. |
| `defaultValues` | `Partial<NewProductFormValues>` | empty, USD, 250 licenses | Starting `cover` (a `File`), `name`, `price` (a number, or `null` for an empty field), `currency`, `licenses`, `tags`, `tiers` and `tierPrices` (tier prices set by hand; the others follow the price). Without it the name and price open empty (their placeholders show) with no cover or tags, on Figma's USD, 250 licenses and the tiers off; `sampleNewProductValues` is the filled sample (Fleet UI kit, dark at $49.00 with three tags). |
| `currencies` | `NewProductCurrency[]` | `sampleNewProductCurrencies` | `{ value, symbol, decimals?, min?, max? }`: the picker shows the code, the field the sign; `decimals` (2) formats the amount, `min` (1) and `max` (100,000) bound the price. |
| `currency` / `onCurrencyChange` | `string` / `(currency: string) => void` | – | Controlled currency. |
| `payoutShare` | `number` | `0.95` | Your share of a sale: "You keep 95%", and the amount in the confirmation. |
| `coverTypes` | `string[]` | `["image/png"]` | Accepted MIME types (the picker's `accept`, the check and the hint's "PNG"). |
| `coverSize` | `{ width: number; height: number }` | `{ width: 1600, height: 1200 }` | The recommended size, in the hint; smaller images get a warning. |
| `coverMaxBytes` | `number` | `10_000_000` | The largest cover file. |
| `maxLicenses` | `number` | `9999` | The stepper's upper bound. |
| `maxTags` | `number` | `5` | "Add" hides at the limit. |
| `tiers` | `NewProductTier[]` | `sampleNewProductTiers` | `{ value, label, multiplier }`, one price each when the switch is on; the line under the switch names them. `[]` hides the panel. |
| `tiersLocked` | `boolean` | `false` | The switch calls `onUpgrade` instead of turning on. |
| `onUpgrade` | `() => void` | – | Called by the locked switch. |
| `draftSavedAt` | `Date \| number \| null` | two minutes before mount | When the draft was last saved; the footer counts from it ("Draft saved 2 min ago"), `null` says "Not saved yet". |
| `onSaveDraft` | `(values: NewProductFormValues) => void \| Promise<void>` | – | Called by "Save draft" with the values as they are (nothing is validated). While a returned promise is pending the footer says "Saving draft…"; a rejection says the save failed. |
| `storeUrl` | `string` | `"mebius.design/p"` | The confirmation shows `<storeUrl>/<the name as a slug>`. |
| `onSubmit` | `(values: NewProductFormValues) => void \| Promise<void>` | – | Called by Publish once everything is valid, with `price` a number, `licenses` within bounds and `tierPrices` filled when `tiers` is on. While a returned promise is pending the button says "Publishing…"; then the confirmation shows; a rejection keeps the form with an error line. |
| `onClose` | `() => void` | – | Called by the × and by Done on the confirmation (Done only shows when it's set). Without it the × puts the default values back. |
| `autoFocus` | `boolean` | `false` | Focuses the name field on mount, as a dialog would. |

Other props go to the `<form>` (`className` and `style` are merged). The file also exports the sample data (`sampleNewProductValues`, `sampleNewProductCurrencies`, `sampleNewProductTiers`) and the types `NewProductFormProps`, `NewProductFormValues`, `NewProductCurrency` and `NewProductTier`. "Add another" on the confirmation opens a blank product.

## Notes

- Figma: the form is "Modal" (1891:83399) inside "Stage / 03 New product" (1891:83397), section "Premium ($14)" on page "Page 7". The stage (canvas, accent glow) is demo-only; the block is the dialog, `w-full max-w-[540px]`; in containers under 500px the cover goes full width above the fields, under 440px the tier prices stack and the footer note takes its own line.
- Installs Geist (`forms-pro-font-geist`) and Geist Mono (`forms-pro-font-geist-mono`, the amounts, the license count, the link and the Enter key hint).
- Colours come from the Forms Pro theme (`form-surface`, `form-surface-2`, `form-surface-3`, `form-border-strong`, `form-accent`, `form-pro`, `form-pro-soft`, `form-on-accent`, `form-success`, `form-warning`, `form-inverse`…), so re-theming the `--form-*` variables re-skins it. Errors use `form-danger` (`#dc2626` light, `#f87171` dark; Figma draws no error state and its variables have no red yet), the small-cover warning `form-warning`. The dialog sets a few colours of its own on its root, overridable through `style`: the Pro badge's label (`--product-pro-ink`), the dialog's and the currency menu's shadows (`--product-shadow`, `--product-menu-shadow`) and the Enter key on Publish (`--product-kbd`, `--product-kbd-line`, `--product-kbd-ink`).
- Light by default and dark under your app's `.dark` class: the form sets its own color-scheme, and its palette holds light-dark() pairs from the Figma light `2713:11048` and dark `1891:83397` frames (the theme's tokens switch with the same class). The light stage has no accent glow. The light frame draws Publish's Enter hint in black at a few percent, which barely shows on the dark button; here it stays in the button's text colour, as in dark.
- The cover is a real file input behind its label, so it works with the keyboard and screen readers; the preview is an object URL, revoked when the cover changes. The currency picker is a Radix Select, the switch a Radix Switch (Space toggles it), the license count an `<input role="spinbutton">`. Errors are linked to their fields with `aria-describedby`, and a visually hidden `aria-live` region says "Saving draft…", "Publishing…" and "… is published."
- The dashed strokes (the cover box, 5 on 4 off; "Add", 3 on 3 off) are drawn as SVG from Figma's own fitting, measured from its renders: every side dashed on its own and symmetric, the first dash centred 0.6 × radius from the corner, the corners dashed by angle. A CSS dashed border can't match it.
- With `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` nothing animates; the server render is the final state too.
- Differences from Figma: Figma draws only the filled dialog, so the picked cover (preview, remove, name and size, warnings), the drag-over state, the tag field and the tags' remove buttons, the currency menu, the tier prices, the errors, saving, publishing, the published and blank states and the focus rings are designed here in the dialog's own style. The focused price field keeps Figma's accent border without the 3px accent shadow its frame carries (Figma's render shows none).

A four-step "New project" modal in split panes. The steps pane on the left tracks where you are (the current step raised, finished steps checked, earlier steps clickable) and shows the selected team's plan usage as a segmented meter. The right pane holds the step: Details (a name, a URL path that follows the name until you edit it, a team select, Private / Public), Repository (the repository to import, its branch and root directory), Environment (key / value rows you add and remove) and Review (every value, each with an Edit link back to its step). Continue validates the step and moves on, Enter in a field does the same, and "Create project" sends everything to `onSubmit`, then shows a confirmation with the project URL and a copy button. Animated with anime.js: the modal rises in, the step highlight glides between steps, each step slides in while the pane eases to its new height, radio dots pop, the plan meter fills, an invalid step shakes and the success check draws itself.

## Usage

With the sample teams and repositories, the fields empty:

```tsx
import { NewProjectForm } from "@/components/new-project-form"

export function Example() {
  return <NewProjectForm onSubmit={(values) => console.log(values)} />
}
```

In a dialog, with your own teams, repositories and starting values and an async `onSubmit`:

```tsx
"use client"


import { NewProjectForm, type NewProjectFormValues, type NewProjectOption, type NewProjectTeam } from "@/components/new-project-form"

const teams: NewProjectTeam[] = [
  { value: "acme", label: "Acme Inc.", plan: { name: "Starter plan", used: 4, limit: 5 } },
  { value: "acme-labs", label: "Acme Labs", plan: { name: "Scale plan", used: 31 } },
]

const repositories: NewProjectOption[] = [
  { value: "acme/web", label: "web", description: "acme · updated 12 minutes ago" },
  { value: "acme/docs", label: "docs", description: "acme · updated yesterday" },
]

async function createProject(values: NewProjectFormValues) {
  const response = await fetch("/api/projects", {
    method: "POST",
    headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
    body: JSON.stringify(values),
  })
  // a rejected promise keeps the form open and shows "Couldn't create the project. Try again."
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Create failed: ${response.status}`)
}

export function Example({ onClose }: { onClose: () => void }) {
  return (
    <NewProjectForm
      title="New site"
      urlPrefix="acme.app/"
      teams={teams}
      repositories={repositories}
      defaultValues={{ name: "", slug: "", team: "acme", repository: "acme/web", env: [] }}
      onSubmit={createProject}
      onCancel={onClose}
      onClose={onClose}
      onUpgrade={(team) => window.open(`/billing?team=${team.value}`)}
    />
  )
}
```

Owning the step (for a URL per step, or analytics):

