## Notes

- Figma: the form is "Modal" (1891:85610) inside "Stage / 13 Cron job" (1891:85609), section "Premium ($14)" on page "Page 7". The stage (black with a #151515 hairline, #f4f5f7 with an #ededed one in the light frame) is demo-only; the block is the dialog, `w-full max-w-[560px]`. In narrow containers the five fields and the segments shrink with it, and the footer note wraps above the buttons when they don't fit beside it.
- Installs Geist (`forms-pro-font-geist`) and Geist Mono (`forms-pro-font-geist-mono`: the method, path, cron fields, runs and expression).
- Re-skin it through the CSS variables on the form (a `style` or a CSS rule on `[data-slot="cron-job-form"]`): `--cron-surface`, `--cron-sunken`, `--cron-raised`, `--cron-selected`, `--cron-line`, `--cron-divider`, `--cron-text`, `--cron-text-2`, `--cron-text-3`, `--cron-focus` (the focused field's border), `--cron-accent`, `--cron-on-accent`, `--cron-field-ring`, `--cron-ring`, `--cron-danger`, `--cron-success`, `--cron-warning`, `--cron-shadow`, `--cron-menu-shadow`. The menus portal to `<body>` and take the values the form has, and its light or dark.
- Light by default and dark under your app's `.dark` class: the form sets its own color-scheme, and its palette holds light-dark() pairs from the Figma light `2713:12966` and dark `1891:85609` frames.
- Cron rules are Vixie cron's: when both day and weekday are restricted, a day matching either runs; 0 and 7 are Sunday; `5/15` steps from 5 to the end of the range. Runs are computed in the chosen time zone, looking up to eight years ahead; a wall-clock time a daylight saving change skips is skipped. A schedule with no run in that window (`0 0 31 2 *`) is called out under the fields and refused on submit.
- Keyboard: arrow keys move between the schedule segments; Up / Down step a numeric cron field (wrapping within its range), Space jumps to the next field, Backspace in an empty field goes back, and pasting a whole expression into any field fills all five. A visually hidden `aria-live` region announces "Creating…" and the confirmation.
- With `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` nothing animates; the server render is the final state too.
- Differences from Figma: Figma draws only the filled dialog, so the errors, the creating and created states, the open menus and the focus rings are designed here in the dialog's own style (errors in red, `#ff6166`, `#dc2626` in light). Figma's selected segment is Custom over an expression that is also the Weekdays preset: the segment is kept as its own value, so both are shown as drawn. The weekday field's lighter border is the focused field (focused fields also get the series' 3px ring, white at 8% in dark and dark ink at 8% in light). The method is a menu (Figma draws "POST" as text). The tag reads "beta" with even padding (Figma's text is " beta", with a leading space). The summary's clock is green while the schedule runs, as drawn, amber when it never runs and grey while it can't be read (with every field empty, as the form opens, it asks for a schedule: "Write the five fields, or pick a preset."). "Next runs" are read from the schedule: the sample reproduces Figma's from Tuesday 27 October 2026, 15:00 in Jakarta, the moment the demo passes as `now` (`sampleCronJobNow`).

An 8-bit checkout in Silkscreen and VT323. A purple header with the step ("2/3") sits over Figma's dither band; below it the order's lines with their colour swatches and prices, a dashed rule, the card number (spaced in fours as you type, checked with Luhn), the expiry (`MM/YY`, the slash added for you) and a masked CVC, and a gold-framed toggle that adds the PRO pass. The gold pay bar reads its amount from the order ("PAY $80", "PAY $109" with the pass) and pays with a click or Enter: invalid fields turn red with a message under them, paying lights a three-square loader, and the paid checkout shows a receipt with a pixel check, the amount, the card's last four digits and the lines it paid for, the header moving on to the next step; DONE brings the checkout back. Animated with anime.js in pixel steps: the checkout drops in while the dither band builds up from the bottom and the lines step in from the left, the swatches pop, the star twinkles every few seconds while it's on screen and spins when the pass goes in, the amount counts to the new total, an invalid payment shakes the checkout and its errors flicker in, paying scans a light across the band, and the receipt draws its check square by square.

## Usage

With the sample order, the card fields empty:

```tsx
import { PixelCheckoutForm } from "@/components/pixel-checkout-form"

export function Example() {
  return <PixelCheckoutForm onSubmit={(values) => console.log(values)} />
}
```

With your cart, your currency, a controlled add-on and your payment API:

```tsx
"use client"

import * as React from "react"

import { PixelCheckoutForm, type PixelCheckoutFormValues, type PixelCheckoutItem } from "@/components/pixel-checkout-form"

const cart: PixelCheckoutItem[] = [
  { id: "sprite-pack", name: "Sprite pack", price: 24, color: "#ff6b9a" },
  { id: "tile-set", name: "Dungeon tile set", price: 18.5, color: "#7ce0ff" },
]

const euros = new Intl.NumberFormat("de-DE", { style: "currency", currency: "EUR" })

async function pay(values: PixelCheckoutFormValues) {
  // send the card to your payment provider's tokenizer, never to your own server
  const response = await fetch("/api/checkout", {
    method: "POST",
    headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
    body: JSON.stringify({ items: cart.map((item) => item.id), addOn: values.addOn, total: values.total }),
  })
  // a throw keeps the checkout and says the payment didn't go through
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Payment failed: ${response.status}`)
}

export function Example({ onFinished }: { onFinished: () => void }) {
  const [withPass, setWithPass] = React.useState(true)

  return (
    <PixelCheckoutForm
      step={3}
      steps={4}
      items={cart}
      addOn={{ label: "Add the soundtrack", price: 9 }}
      addOnSelected={withPass}
      onAddOnChange={setWithPass}
      formatPrice={(amount) => euros.format(amount)}
      labels={{ pay: "PAY" }}
      onSubmit={pay}
      successDescription={(values) => `Paid ${euros.format(values.total)}. Your downloads are ready.`}
      onDone={onFinished}
    />
  )
}
```

## Props

| Prop | Type | Default | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `title` | `string` | `"CHECKOUT"` | The header's heading (also the form's name). |
| `step` / `steps` | `number` | `2` / `3` | The header's "2/3". Once paid it shows the next step. |
| `items` | `PixelCheckoutItem[]` | `samplePixelCheckoutItems` | `{ id, name, price, color }`: the order's lines and their swatch colours. The total is read from them. |
| `addOn` | `PixelCheckoutAddOn \| null` | `samplePixelCheckoutAddOn` | `{ label, price }`: the gold-framed toggle (Figma's "Add PRO pass, all future kits", +$29). `null` leaves the row out. |
| `defaultValues` | `Partial<PixelCheckoutFormDefaults>` | an empty card, the add-on off | Starting values, merged over an empty card number, expiry and CVC (their placeholders show) with Figma's add-on off; `samplePixelCheckoutValues` is the filled Figma sample (the `4242 4242 4242 4242` test card, `12/28`, CVC `424`). Without `onDone`, DONE goes back to them. |
| `addOnSelected` / `onAddOnChange` | `boolean` / `(selected: boolean) => void` | – | Control the add-on (e.g. to keep it in step with your cart). |
| `onSubmit` | `(values: PixelCheckoutFormValues) => void \| Promise<void>` | – | Called once the card is valid. While a returned promise is pending the pay bar says "PAYING" and the fields are locked; a rejection keeps everything and says the payment didn't go through. |
| `formatPrice` | `(amount: number) => string` | `$80`, `$12.50` | Writes every amount: the lines, the add-on, the pay bar and the receipt. |
| `labels` | `Partial<PixelCheckoutFormLabels>` | Figma's | `cardNumber` (`"CARD"`), `expiry` (`"EXP"`), `cvc` (`"CVC"`), `pay` (the word before the amount, `"PAY"`) and `done` (`"DONE"`). |
| `successTitle` | `string` | `"PAYMENT DONE"` | The receipt's heading. |
| `successDescription` | `(values: PixelCheckoutFormValues) => ReactNode` | `$80 paid with the card ending 4242` | The line under it. |
| `onDone` | `() => void` | – | Called by DONE once paid. Without it, DONE brings the checkout back to its starting values. |

`PixelCheckoutFormValues` is `{ cardNumber, expiry, cvc, addOn, total }`: the card number's digits without spaces, the expiry as `MM/YY`, the CVC's digits, whether the add-on is in and the total read from the order. Other props go to the `<form>` (`className` and `style` are merged, `ref` reaches the form). The file also exports the sample data (`samplePixelCheckoutItems`, `samplePixelCheckoutAddOn`, `samplePixelCheckoutValues`) and the types `PixelCheckoutFormProps`, `PixelCheckoutFormValues`, `PixelCheckoutFormDefaults`, `PixelCheckoutFormLabels`, `PixelCheckoutItem` and `PixelCheckoutAddOn`.

## Notes

- Validation on submit: a card number of 13 to 19 digits that passes the Luhn check, an expiry `MM/YY` with a month from 01 to 12 that hasn't passed, and a CVC of 3 or 4 digits. Invalid fields get a red border and a message under them (linked with `aria-describedby`), the first one is focused and the checkout shakes; a message goes away as soon as its field is fixed.
- Typing: the card number keeps the caret after the digit you typed while the spaces move; the expiry adds the slash after the month, writes a first digit above 1 as a month (`4` → `04`), and Backspace over the slash takes the month's last digit with it. The fields carry the `cc-number`, `cc-exp` and `cc-csc` autocomplete hints, so browsers and password managers fill them in.
- The add-on is a Radix checkbox (Space toggles it); checked, its row turns gold and the amount counts to the new total.
- This is the checkout's look and behaviour, not a payment integration: for real payments send the card to your provider's tokenizer (or render the provider's own fields in these boxes) rather than to your server.
- Light by default and dark under your app's `.dark` class (the form sets its own color-scheme; its palette holds light-dark() pairs from the Figma light 2713:19891 and dark 1891:92534 frames). The violet header and band, the gold pay bar and the swatches are the same in both; the hard shadow is violet in dark and near-black in light.
- Re-skin it through the CSS variables on the form: `--px-panel`, `--px-edge`, `--px-accent` (the header and the dither band), `--px-on-accent` (the header's text), `--px-shadow`, `--px-field`, `--px-ink`, `--px-muted`, `--px-gold` (the add-on and the pay bar), `--px-gold-ink` (prices, the add-on's star and price, the focus outlines), `--px-pass`, `--px-on-gold`, `--px-success` and `--px-danger`, e.g. `style={{ "--px-accent": "#ff6b9a", "--px-shadow": "light-dark(#1c1d21, #ff6b9a)" } as React.CSSProperties}` (a light-dark() pair re-skins both themes, a plain colour sets both). The swatches take each item's `color`.
