A "New User Account" dialog from a retro desktop OS. Dark draws every edge as an inner-shadow bevel: raised buttons and tabs, sunken fields, a gradient title bar; light draws it flat, a white window with a pale line and a soft shadow, grey buttons and fields with faint bevels. Three working tabs: Profile (a picture you browse for, shown pixelated, with its file name; full name; a user name that follows the full name until you edit it, "Yusuf Hadi" → `yhadi`; e-mail; a department list; a password shown as asterisks like classic dialogs; two option check boxes), General (a description, the account type, and the home folder and groups read from the user name and department) and Security ("Password never expires", off while the user must change the password, and the lockout). OK and Apply validate the fields (red label and message, the first one focused, the window shakes) and call `onSubmit`; while it runs the status bar shows the progress, one of 20 segments per 5%, from the steps you give or from what your request reports through `progress(64, "Creating home folder...")`. Apply keeps the dialog open and greys out until something changes; OK ends on a message box with "OK" and "New user...". The title bar's buttons roll the dialog up, widen it to its container and close it. Animated with anime.js in steps, like an old screen: the window snaps open, the title bar draws in, tabs and rows redraw one by one, the robot's pixels fill in, progress segments light up while the percentage counts, checks pop, pages redraw when you switch tabs, the window rolls up and widens in steps, and the message box snaps open.

