The anime.js plumbing every chart card shares: `useReducedMotion` (the system setting), `useIsomorphicLayoutEffect`, `useAnimeScope` (a scope reverted on unmount), `useChartEntrance` (a paused timeline that plays once the chart scrolls into view), `useInView` (whether the chart is on screen, to pause ambient loops), `useCountUp` (numbers that count to their new value) and `releaseStyles` (an onComplete that hands the element back to its classes).

## Usage

An entrance and a counting total for your own card:

```tsx
"use client"

import { stagger } from "animejs"
import * as React from "react"

import { ChartCard } from "@/components/ui/chart-card"
import { useChartEntrance, useCountUp } from "@/hooks/use-chart-motion"

const bars = [40, 72, 56, 88, 64]
const usd = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString("en-US", { style: "currency", currency: "USD" })

export function Example({ total = 12840.5 }: { total?: number }) {
  const root = React.useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null)
  const totalRef = React.useRef<HTMLParagraphElement>(null)

  useChartEntrance(root, (timeline) => {
    timeline.add(root.current!.querySelectorAll("[data-bar]"), { height: { from: 0 }, duration: 900, ease: "outExpo", delay: stagger(60) })
  })
  useCountUp(totalRef, total, usd)

  return (
    <ChartCard ref={root} className="w-[320px]">
      <p ref={totalRef} className="typo-heading-02-semibold">
        {usd(total)}
      </p>
      <div className="flex h-24 items-end gap-2">
        {bars.map((h, i) => (
          <div key={i} data-bar className="flex-1 rounded-t-lg bg-primary-orange-500" style={{ height: h }} />
        ))}
      </div>
    </ChartCard>
  )
}
```

A transient tween that ends on what the classes already say:

```tsx
"use client"

import { animate } from "animejs"
import * as React from "react"

import { releaseStyles, useIsomorphicLayoutEffect, useReducedMotion } from "@/hooks/use-chart-motion"

export function Example({ open }: { open: boolean }) {
  const panel = React.useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null)
  const reduced = useReducedMotion()

  useIsomorphicLayoutEffect(() => {
    if (!panel.current || reduced) return
    animate(panel.current, { opacity: [open ? 0 : 1, open ? 1 : 0], duration: 250, onComplete: releaseStyles("opacity") })
  }, [open, reduced])

  return <div ref={panel} className={open ? "opacity-100" : "opacity-0"} />
}
```

An ambient loop that rests while the card is scrolled away:

```tsx
"use client"

import { animate } from "animejs"
import * as React from "react"

import { useInView, useReducedMotion } from "@/hooks/use-chart-motion"

export function Example() {
  const root = React.useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null)
  const glow = React.useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null)
  const inView = useInView(root)
  const reduced = useReducedMotion()

  React.useEffect(() => {
    if (!glow.current || reduced || !inView) return
    const breath = animate(glow.current, { opacity: [1, 0.65], duration: 2400, ease: "inOutSine", loop: true, alternate: true })
    return () => {
      breath.revert()
    }
  }, [inView, reduced])

  return (
    <div ref={root} className="relative h-24 w-[320px]">
      <div ref={glow} className="absolute inset-0 rounded-2xl bg-primary-blue-100" />
    </div>
  )
}
```

## API

| Export | Signature | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `useReducedMotion` | `() => boolean` | `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`; `false` on the server. |
| `useIsomorphicLayoutEffect` | same as `useLayoutEffect` | `useLayoutEffect` in the browser (first frames before paint), `useEffect` on the server. |
| `useAnimeScope` | `(root: RefObject<HTMLElement \| null>) => RefObject<Scope \| null>` | Animations made with `scope.current.execute(() => animate(…))` are reverted on unmount. |
| `useChartEntrance` | `(root, build: (timeline) => void \| (() => void), { amount = 0.25 }?)` | Builds a paused timeline, applies every tween's first frame before paint, plays it once `amount` of `root` is visible. Skipped with reduced motion. Reverted (plus your cleanup) on unmount. |
| `useInView` | `(root: RefObject<HTMLElement \| null>) => boolean` | Whether any of `root` is on screen (an IntersectionObserver); `true` until the first observation and on the server. For pausing loops, not for entrances (`useChartEntrance` has its own). |
| `useCountUp` | `(ref, value, format, { enabled = true, duration = 600 }?)` | Animates the element's text from the previous value to `value`; React still renders the final text. `enabled: false` stops a running count. |
| `releaseStyles` | `(...properties: string[]) => (animation) => void` | onComplete helper: removes those inline properties from the targets. |

## Notes

- The pattern behind every chart: React renders the final state (also on the server and with reduced motion); anime.js only animates the way there, starting from the previous state, and hands the element back to its classes when done.
- Don't use `utils.cleanInlineStyles` for interruptible tweens: it restores the inline value from before the animation, which can be a frame of the animation it interrupted. `releaseStyles` clears the properties instead.
- Needs `animejs` 4.

State that a parent can control (`value` + `onChange`) or leave to the component (`defaultValue`), the pattern Radix uses. The chart cards, the segmented control and the tabs use it for every value you may want to own: range, mask, selected tab.

## Usage

```tsx
"use client"

import { useControllableState } from "@/hooks/use-controllable-state"

export function Toggle({ pressed, defaultPressed = false, onPressedChange }: { pressed?: boolean; defaultPressed?: boolean; onPressedChange?: (pressed: boolean) => void }) {
  const [on, setOn] = useControllableState({ value: pressed, defaultValue: defaultPressed, onChange: onPressedChange })

  return (
    <button type="button" aria-pressed={on} onClick={() => setOn(!on)} className="typo-label-02-medium text-fg-loud">
      {on ? "On" : "Off"}
