The anime.js plumbing every form block shares: `useReducedMotion` (the system setting) and `prefersReducedMotion` (the same, read once in an event handler), `useIsomorphicLayoutEffect`, `useAnimeScope` (a scope reverted on unmount), `useFormEntrance` (a paused timeline that plays once the form scrolls into view), `useInView` (whether the form is on screen, to pause ambient loops), `useCountUp` (numbers that count to their new value), `shake` (an invalid submit), `blink` (a two-state ease for blinking loops) and `releaseStyles` (an onComplete that hands the element back to its classes).

## Usage

An entrance, a counting total and a shake on an invalid submit:

```tsx
"use client"

import { stagger } from "animejs"
import * as React from "react"

import { shake, useCountUp, useFormEntrance } from "@/hooks/use-form-motion"

const usd = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString("en-US", { style: "currency", currency: "USD" })

export function Example({ total = 129 }: { total?: number }) {
  const root = React.useRef<HTMLFormElement>(null)
  const totalRef = React.useRef<HTMLParagraphElement>(null)
  const [email, setEmail] = React.useState("")

  useFormEntrance(root, (timeline) => {
    timeline
      .add(root.current!, { opacity: { from: 0 }, y: { from: 12 }, duration: 500, ease: "outExpo" })
      .add(root.current!.querySelectorAll("[data-field]"), { opacity: { from: 0 }, y: { from: 8 }, duration: 400, ease: "outQuad", delay: stagger(50) }, 120)
  })
  useCountUp(totalRef, total, usd)

  return (
    <form
      ref={root}
      noValidate
      className="flex w-[360px] flex-col gap-3 rounded-2xl bg-form-surface p-5 font-form-geist text-form-text"
      onSubmit={(event) => {
        event.preventDefault()
        if (!email.includes("@")) shake(root.current)
      }}
    >
      <p ref={totalRef} className="text-2xl font-semibold">
        {usd(total)}
      </p>
      <input data-field value={email} onChange={(event) => setEmail(event.target.value)} placeholder="you@company.com" className="rounded-md bg-form-surface-2 px-3 py-2 text-sm" />
      <button data-field type="submit" className="rounded-md bg-form-inverse px-3 py-2 text-sm text-form-on-inverse">
        Continue
      </button>
    </form>
  )
}
```

A transient tween that ends on what the classes already say:

```tsx
"use client"

import { animate } from "animejs"
import * as React from "react"

import { releaseStyles, useIsomorphicLayoutEffect, useReducedMotion } from "@/hooks/use-form-motion"

export function Example({ open }: { open: boolean }) {
  const panel = React.useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null)
  const reduced = useReducedMotion()

  useIsomorphicLayoutEffect(() => {
    if (!panel.current || reduced) return
    animate(panel.current, { opacity: [open ? 0 : 1, open ? 1 : 0], duration: 250, onComplete: releaseStyles("opacity") })
  }, [open, reduced])

  return <div ref={panel} className={open ? "opacity-100" : "opacity-0"} />
}
```

An ambient loop that rests while the form is scrolled away:

```tsx
"use client"

import { animate } from "animejs"
import * as React from "react"

import { blink, useInView, useReducedMotion } from "@/hooks/use-form-motion"

export function Example() {
  const root = React.useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null)
  const caret = React.useRef<HTMLSpanElement>(null)
  const inView = useInView(root)
  const reduced = useReducedMotion()

  React.useEffect(() => {
    if (!caret.current || !inView || reduced) return
    const loop = animate(caret.current, { opacity: [1, 0], duration: 1060, ease: blink, loop: true })
    return () => {
      loop.revert()
    }
  }, [inView, reduced])

  return (
    <div ref={root} className="font-form-vt323 text-xl text-[#9dff9d]">
      &gt; READY<span ref={caret}>_</span>
    </div>
  )
}
```

## API

- `useReducedMotion(): boolean` and `prefersReducedMotion(): boolean`
- `useIsomorphicLayoutEffect`: `useLayoutEffect` in the browser, `useEffect` on the server
- `useAnimeScope(root: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>): RefObject<Scope | null>`
- `useFormEntrance(root, build: (timeline: Timeline) => void | (() => void), { amount = 0.25 })`
- `useInView(root): boolean`
- `useCountUp(ref, value: number, format: (value: number) => string, { enabled = true, duration = 600 })`
- `shake(target: Element | null | undefined, { distance = 6, duration = 420 }): JSAnimation | undefined`
- `blink(t: number): number`: holds the start value for the first half of each iteration and the end value for the second
- `releaseStyles(...properties: string[]): (animation: JSAnimation) => void`

## Notes

- React always renders the final state; anime.js only animates the way there, so server rendering and reduced motion show the finished form.
- `useFormEntrance` applies every tween's first frame before paint, so nothing flashes, and reverts the timeline on unmount.
- `shake` and every entrance do nothing when the system asks for reduced motion.
- anime.js 4.5 dropped string eases like `"steps(1)"`, and `steps(1)` with `alternate: true` never leaves the start value: blink with `ease: blink`, `loop: true` and twice the on-time as `duration`.

The submit status every form block shows: `idle`, `submitting` while your `onSubmit` promise is pending, then `success`. When `onSubmit` throws or rejects, the status goes back to `idle` and `submit` rethrows, so the form can show what went wrong.

## Usage

```tsx
"use client"

import * as React from "react"

import { useFormSubmit } from "@/hooks/use-form-submit"

type Values = { email: string }

export function Example({ onSubmit }: { onSubmit?: (values: Values) => Promise<void> }) {
  const [email, setEmail] = React.useState("")
  const [error, setError] = React.useState<string | null>(null)
  const { status, submit, reset } = useFormSubmit(onSubmit)

  return (
    <form
      className="flex flex-col gap-2 font-form-geist text-sm text-form-text"
      onSubmit={async (event) => {
        event.preventDefault()
        setError(null)
        try {
          await submit({ email })
        } catch {
          setError("Something went wrong. Try again.")
        }
      }}
    >
      <input value={email} onChange={(event) => setEmail(event.target.value)} type="email" required className="rounded-md bg-form-surface-2 px-3 py-2" />
      {error && <p role="alert" className="text-xs text-red-400">{error}</p>}
      {status === "success" ? (
        <button type="button" onClick={reset}>Sent. Send another</button>
      ) : (
        <button type="submit" disabled={status === "submitting"}>{status === "submitting" ? "Sending…" : "Send"}</button>
      )}
    </form>
  )
}
```

## API

`useFormSubmit<T>(onSubmit?: (values: T) => void | Promise<void>) => { status: "idle" | "submitting" | "success"; submit: (values: T) => Promise<void>; reset: () => void }`

## Notes

- Without `onSubmit` the status goes straight to `success`, so a block still shows its whole flow in a prototype.
- The status is kept when the component re-renders with a new `onSubmit`; call `reset()` to start over.

